{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he called to the elders of the people.</b> Behold, Moses our master, peace be upon him, acted wisely in his mission, carrying it out with deliberation, for his heart was frightened and in dread lest some stumbling occur in the matter that is the purpose of everything and the essence of the world — and the earth had already seized ropes, as it were, lest Israel not accept the Torah, Heaven forbid. He acted wisely, peace be upon him, and did not address the entirety of the people directly; rather, he called the elders and seated them before him, with all Israel together hearing the words of Moses. But he presented the matter before the elders, and this accomplished that the people would not begin to answer Moses — only the elders would, not the generality of the people. For he was concerned lest someone from the people give an unfitting answer, Heaven forbid, whereas with the elders he was confident that their answer would be fitting; and after the elders would answer fittingly, the people would incline after them. And even if they were to gnash their teeth, Heaven forbid, each and every elder would speak to the members of his own family. Perhaps, however, since they are children of the living El, Moses' wisdom became known to them — for he feared refusal, Heaven forbid — and they removed themselves from suspicion and did not allow the elders to answer first; instead all the people answered together: ״All that HaShem has spoken we will do,״ of our own complete knowledge and simple will, and the elders' answer did not precede the answer of all the people of HaShem. And so Israel showed the strength of their unity and the equality of their will toward our Elohim, for six hundred thousand spoke with identical speech, as it says ״yachdav״ (together) — not one of them was late, not one preceded his fellow, not one changed to answer in a different formulation. Happy is the world that has this nation within it! And from this we may understand the prophet's words (Jeremiah 2:2): ״כֹּה אָמַר ה׳ זָכַרְתִּי לָךְ חֶסֶד נְעוּרַיִךְ אַהֲבַת כְּלוּלֹתָיִךְ״ — meaning, his saying ״kelulotayich״ (your affiances) refers to the answer they gave, all the people together, for they made two generalities in love: first, a fitting answer from all of them, with not even one who was not willing in the matter; second, that they were equal together in the voice of the answer and in the language of the answer, in the manner we have written." "<b>et all, etc.</b> He intended in saying <b>et all</b> that he did not diminish from what was told him at all, without any reduction; and his saying <b>these</b> means he did not add to speak anything beyond what HaShem had said to him (Exodus 19:6) — only the words."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:01.533332298Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}